#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088745} :activity/id #uuid "d16c173d-d5fc-51c9-adf4-fe3999473ab4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049622 :topic/id #uuid "0613a6d7-8b7b-5bc2-9f9a-e72e48c2994a" :topic/math-academy-id 814} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088500 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088990 :tutorial/content "Let's consider our first example once more.\n\n![](images/0c/0c53b8e5d2124d13d92dd81ab4868c6cc128da40eabfe51e684306bb0bbf0457.png)\n\nYou might be wondering how we can tell that the vertex coordinates are $(2,1)$ just from looking at the equation.\n\nTo see this, let's label the two terms in our parabola formula:\n\n$$\ny=\\underbrace{(x-2)^2}_{\\textrm{quadratic part}}+\\underbrace{1}_{\\textrm{constant}}\n$$\n\nNow, note the following:\n\n- The quadratic part $(x-2)^2$ is a *squared* quantity. Therefore, it can only be *positive* or *zero*.\n- Moreover, the quadratic part *equals* zero when $x=2$.\n- Since the constant part $(1)$ does not vary with $x$, the entire expression is *minimized* when $x=2$. Therefore, the $x$-coordinate of the minimum point (i.e., the vertex) is $2$.\n- When $x=2$, the quadratic part is zero, giving $y=0^2+1 = 1$. Therefore, the $y$-coordinate of the vertex is $1$.\n\nFollowing this logic, we conclude that the coordinates of the vertex are $(2,1)$.\n\nFinally, it's possible to generalize this argument to any parabola. For $a < 0$, the coordinates $(h,k)$ give the maximum of the parabola." :tutorial/id #uuid "d30b5d5a-0248-511a-9f7f-dfff00438889" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9115 :tutorial/title "Why Does Vertex Form Give the Coordinates of the Vertex?"} :step/id #uuid "c203d163-0475-523c-b583-dea9d27a9184" :step/math-academy-id 136678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088690 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087803 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087631 :question/id #uuid "d4c1e4ea-da29-5027-bfc3-f909f9181cd0" :question/math-academy-id "e-7479"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "97f63d2a-8297-5034-96e0-ecae4f62c5a8" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088182} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088183}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Minimum or Maximum Value of a Parabola Given in Vertex Form"} :step/id #uuid "a04762c2-835a-5511-af4c-a7041c880b62" :step/math-academy-id 27764 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088500}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088745 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088870 :tutorial/content "Up to now, all the parabolas we've seen have been written in **standard form**.\n\n$$\ny = ax^2+bx+c\n$$\n\nWe can also write the equation of a parabola in the so-called **vertex form,** given by\n\n$$\ny=a(x-h)^2 + k\n$$\n\nWhen a parabola is written in vertex form, the pair $(h,k)$ gives the coordinates of its vertex.\n\nFor example, consider the following parabola:\n\n$$\ny=(x-2)^2 +1\n$$\n\nThis parabola is written in vertex form with $a = 1, h=2$ and $k=1$. Therefore, the vertex of the parabola is at $(2,1)$.\n\n![](images/0c/0c53b8e5d2124d13d92dd81ab4868c6cc128da40eabfe51e684306bb0bbf0457.png)\n\nIn future lessons, we'll learn how to express any parabola in vertex form. For now, let's get some practice working with this form." :tutorial/id #uuid "a7456ef9-dd1e-56e5-a24f-111ba9692968" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1648 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "cbd152b4-52af-56c9-8eab-5981824f4582" :step/math-academy-id 4592 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088746}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088746 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087831 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087436 :question/id #uuid "c9e4edff-b04f-5f6c-8066-43cae6a653e6" :question/math-academy-id "e-1466"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61e9b29c-326d-576c-b998-ab975492ed85" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048598} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049919}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088180}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vertex of a Parabola Given in Vertex Form"} :step/id #uuid "e1a2056c-2d10-5b6e-adcd-9ea2956bbe34" :step/math-academy-id 4593 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088836}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088836 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087885 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087445 :question/id #uuid "8b64498e-61fb-5eab-a97a-b321be8360ef" :question/math-academy-id "e-1795"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4018be9-18ab-5854-87d6-149238ab6f53" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088181}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Axis of Symmetry of a Parabola Given in Vertex Form"} :step/id #uuid "c556f464-3107-5978-b675-fbf5f1c7c267" :step/math-academy-id 6181 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088690}}] :activity/title "The Vertex Form of a Parabola" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087900}]}
